{"id":"5d259c2a-deef-48a5-8970-101ee4634c63","object":"chat.completion","created":1784825521,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['payload']['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['payload']['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    # Build children map\\n    children = {nid: set() for nid in node_map}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            if pid not in children:\\n                children[pid] = set()\\n            children[pid].add(n['id'])\\n    # Determine root (parent is None)\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['parent'] is None:\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n            break\\n    if root_id is None:\\n        raise ValueError('No root node')\\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then by id as tie-breaker\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed_ids = set()\\n    removal_order = []\\n    # Compute initial set of all symbol occurrences (for checking required symbols)\\n    # We'll maintain a count of each symbol among retained nodes\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        s = n['symbol']\\n        symbol_counts[s] = symbol_counts.get(s, 0) + 1\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        # Check if node has any retained children\\n        if any(ch not in removed_ids for ch in children.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        # Tentatively check required symbols after removal\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        # Temporarily decrement count\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 1) - 1\\n        ok = True\\n        for req in required_symbols:\\n            if symbol_counts.get(req, 0) < 1:\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n        if ok:\\n            # Actually remove\\n            removed_ids.add(nid)\\n            removal_order.append(nid)\\n        else:\\n            # Revert count\\n            symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    retained_ids = sorted([n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed_ids])\\n    return {'retained_ids': retained_ids, 'removal_order': removal_order}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to output exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". The value is a Python source string defining `solve(case)`. The function must follow the constraints: use only standard library, no file/network/random etc., return JSON-serializable object, not mutate case.\n\nWe need to implement \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" based on the description. Given a case with nodes (tree) and required_symbols, we need to produce a reduced tree by removing removable nodes while preserving grammar validity? Actually, there's a grammar? The problem mentions \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" but no explicit grammar is given. However, from the fixture, we have nodes with symbols, parent, depth, removable flag, utility. Also required_symbols. The task is to reduce the parse tree by removing removable nodes (tentatively) as long as all required symbols remain represented. Also, \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\"\n\nSo it's a tree reduction algorithm: we have a tree, we want to remove some removable nodes (those with `removable: true`) subject to constraints: root cannot be removed, nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols cannot be removed, nodes that have children that are retained cannot be removed (i.e., if we remove a node, we must remove its entire subtree? Actually, \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That means if a node has any child that is not removed (i.e., retained), then we cannot remove the node. Because removing a node would also remove its subtree? But the text says \"node with retained children\" – if we remove a node, its children become orphaned? In parse tree reduction, typically removing a node means removing that node and connecting its children to its parent? But the description says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" – maybe it's about deleting nodes and possibly replacing with other nodes? However, the instruction \"Never remove a node with retained children\" suggests that if you keep a child, you cannot remove the parent. That implies that removal of a node implies removal of its entire subtree (since children would be removed as well). So it's like pruning: you can only remove a node if all its descendants are also removed (or if it's a leaf). But the text says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we process nodes in decreasing depth (deepest first). For each node that is removable, not root, not required symbol, and currently has no children that are retained (i.e., all its children have been removed already? Actually, we process deeper first, so when we get to a node, its children have already been processed. So if any child was kept, then the node cannot be removed because it would have retained children. If all children were removed, then the node can be removed, provided that after removal, all required symbols are still present somewhere in the tree. Since we remove nodes only after processing deeper nodes, the required symbol condition must be checked globally: after tentatively removing this node, are all required symbols still present in the set of retained nodes? Since we are processing deeper first, we need to consider the cumulative effect.\n\nAlso, we need to return sorted retained IDs (list of IDs of nodes that are kept) and the actual removal order (list of IDs in order they were removed). The removal order is the order in which we decided to remove nodes.\n\nThe algorithm:\n- Build a node lookup by id.\n- Compute children mapping from parent links.\n- Determine initial set of \"retained\" nodes: all nodes? Actually we start with all nodes. Then we will remove some. The removal decision is based on processing nodes in order of decreasing depth, then by node ID as tie-breaker (stable). For each node in that order:\n   - Skip if not removable, or if its id is root, or if its symbol is in required_symbols.\n   - Also skip if it has any child that is currently in retained set (i.e., not removed). Because we cannot remove a node with retained children.\n   - Then tentatively assume we remove it: check if after removal, all required symbols are still present among the retained nodes (which currently includes this node, but we are about to remove it). So we need to check: are there other nodes with the required symbols? For each required symbol, there must be at least one node (other than this one) that has that symbol and is retained (i.e., not removed). Also need to consider that the node itself might be the only one for a required symbol; if so, cannot remove.\n   - If condition holds, then we actually remove it: set a flag that node is removed. Add its id to removal_order list. Remove it from retained set (or just keep a set of removed nodes). The retained set is all nodes minus removed ones.\n- After processing all nodes, we have retained set. Then output sorted retained IDs (should be sorted by id? Probably ascending, but the instruction says \"sorted retained IDs\" – might be sorted by some order? Usually by id. And removal order is the order in which nodes were removed (the order we processed and decided to remove).\n\nNote: \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" Since we process deeper first, children are processed before parent. So when we consider a parent, its children have already been decided: some may be removed, some may be retained. If any child is retained, we cannot remove the parent. That matches.\n\nBut what about nodes that are not leaf but have no children? (i.e., leaf nodes). They can be removed if conditions hold.\n\nWe also need to handle the possibility that the root is not removable (given), required symbols nodes not removable.\n\nAlso need to ensure that we do not mutate case. So we'll create a set of removed_ids, and a dict of nodes from case.\n\nReturn value: JSON-serializable dict with keys? The problem says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So likely return a dict with \"retained_ids\" and \"removal_order\". But the task description just says \"Return one JSON object with exactly one key, `implementation`. Its value must be Python source defining `solve(case)`. The function must return a JSON-serializable object.\" So we decide the return shape. We'll make it {\"retained_ids\": [...], \"removal_order\": [...]}. But we should check the fixture: it shows cases with nodes, but no expected output. The instruction: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So that's the output.\n\nPotential edge cases: If a node is removable but its removal would cause a required symbol to be missing, we skip it. Also, if a node has children that are all removed, we can consider removing it, but need to check required symbols across entire tree.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Build depth map and children map.\n- Sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending (since id is tie-breaker). The id is string? In fixture, ids are \"root\", \"n1\", \"n2\", etc. So string comparison? We need stable tie break. Likely use the id as string, but maybe they expect numeric order of the numeric suffix? The instruction says \"node ID as 